## Global Constraints

- **Backend role gating, verified against the real router files, not assumed — this shapes what buttons exist:**
  - Tasks: `create` and `update`/PATCH and `cancel` are **manager-only** (`require_manager`). `complete` is **rep-only** (`require_rep`) — the dashboard is a manager tool, so there is **no "Complete" button anywhere in this UI**. A task's `status` field is shown read-only; it becomes `COMPLETED` only when a rep completes it from the tablet app (not built yet, out of scope). Building a Complete button here would just 403 on every click.
  - Goals: `create` is **manager-only**. `log-value` (the only way to record a `SALES_VALUE` goal's progress) is **rep-only** (`require_rep`) — there is **no "Log Value" button anywhere in this UI** either, for the same reason. Progress is still viewable (`GET /{id}/progress`, open to both roles) — managers can see where a goal stands, they just can't drive it themselves for `SALES_VALUE` goals (`VISIT_COUNT`/`TASK_COUNT` goals compute automatically and need no manual input from anyone).
  - `list`/`get` on both tasks and goals are open to both roles (manager sees all, filtered server-side).
- **`leaflet-draw`'s real TypeScript surface, verified by installing `leaflet-draw`+`@types/leaflet-draw` and reading the actual `.d.ts` file (not guessed):**
  - `import 'leaflet-draw'` is a side-effect-only import that must come after `import L from 'leaflet'` — it patches the same `L` object with `L.Control.Draw`, `L.Draw.Event`, etc. Both must resolve to the SAME `leaflet` package instance (a single `leaflet` entry in `package.json` — do not let a duplicate get installed).
  - `L.Control.Draw`'s constructor takes `{ draw: {...}, edit: {...} }`. To restrict drawing to polygons only: `draw: { polygon: {}, marker: false, circle: false, circlemarker: false, polyline: false, rectangle: false }` (each non-polygon key must be explicitly `false` — leaving a key `undefined` means "enabled with defaults", not "disabled").
  - The created-shape event is `L.Draw.Event.CREATED` (a string constant `"draw:created"`), and the event object's `.layer` field is a real `L.Polygon` instance (from core Leaflet, not a `Draw.*` namespace class) — call `.toGeoJSON()` on it to get a GeoJSON `Feature`, then take `.geometry` to get the boundary object. The exact TS type of `.geometry` traces through `@types/geojson` (a transitive dependency of `@types/leaflet`); rather than fight for exact structural typing against two loosely-typed community packages, cast through `unknown` to this codebase's own `GeoJSONPolygon` type at this one FFI boundary — a controlled, documented escape hatch, not a type-safety hole that spreads anywhere else.
  - `leaflet-draw` has its own CSS (`leaflet-draw/dist/leaflet.draw.css`) that must be imported or the toolbar renders unstyled/broken — same class of gotcha as `leaflet/dist/leaflet.css` in 7a, get it right the first time.
- **Zone-drawing name prompt uses `window.prompt(...)`, deliberately** — a full modal component is real UI work beyond what a v1 "draw a zone, give it a name" flow needs; matches 7a's login page precedent of picking the smallest reasonable UI for a first pass. `docs/design.md`'s own scope-cut rule explicitly says cut dashboard polish before backend correctness — this is exactly that kind of cut, made deliberately and named as such, not an oversight.
- **`ZonesPage`'s rep-assignment picker has no "unassign" action** — the backend has no `DELETE` endpoint for a `ZoneAssignment` (checked: `zones/router.py` only has `POST /{zone_id}/assignments` and `GET /{zone_id}/assignments`, no delete). Assign-only is the full backend surface; don't build a frontend action with no endpoint behind it.
- Pagination: `tasks.ts`/`goals.ts`/`violations.ts`'s `get*` functions already accept `limit`/`offset` (built in 7a). This plan's list pages use the defaults (50/0) — no pagination UI (page-forward/back controls) is being built, since none of this data is expected to exceed 50 rows in a v1 demo/small-tenant scenario. Note this as a known v1 limitation, not a bug.
- Routing: all new pages are added as children of the existing `<ProtectedRoute><Layout /></ProtectedRoute>` parent route (Task 1 introduces `Layout`), so they inherit auth protection automatically — no page adds its own auth check.
- No new test files beyond what's already established unless a task introduces genuinely new logic worth one (per 7a's calibration) — most of this plan is UI composition over already-tested API client functions, which is exactly the kind of code the plan's own testing philosophy says `build` + the controller's browser check cover, not unit tests.

